#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The acceptance instrument for issues/whnf-sharing-term-growth.md.

Stage 0 of the shared-value whnf round (issues/nbe-conversion-round.md,
docs/nbe-conversion-design.md §13). This is NOT part of `scripts/test` and
NOT part of `scripts/bench`, on purpose:

  * it is not a kernel bench, because the bench suite is zero-dependency and
    cannot call the elaborator, and §12 of the design doc records that three
    hand-built *kernel* reconstructions of this shape all measured linear —
    small terms keep their sharing through `subst.rs`'s depth-zero fast path,
    so the exponent needs the elaborated interpreter's binder depth;
  * it is not a `scripts/test` assertion, because a wall-clock threshold in a
    test rots. The assertion here is a RATIO between adjacent levels, and the
    honest way to use it is to run it deliberately, on one machine, before
    and after, and compare.

Usage (from the repo root; needs `cargo build --release` first):

    scripts/probe-whnf-sharing                  # nest family, N = 1..14
    scripts/probe-whnf-sharing --family both    # plus the negative control
    scripts/probe-whnf-sharing --max 8 --keep   # short sweep, keep the .hthr
    scripts/probe-whnf-sharing --expect linear  # stage 2b's gate

Every `heather` run happens under the CLAUDE.md rail — RLIMIT_AS 8 GiB,
RLIMIT_CPU 120 s, set on the child — which is `ulimit -v 8388608 -t 120`
expressed through `resource.setrlimit`. Nothing here may be run without it:
the a=3 probe on this path once reached ~22 GB and OOM-pressured the host.


THE SHAPE
=========

Probe files are generated, not committed: each one is the DECLARATIONS half
of `lib/certgen/ir_v1.hthr` (everything before the first `theorem`, exactly the cut
`crates/elab/tests/ir_v1.rs` makes) plus a single generated theorem. There is
no import system — a `.hthr` file is a whole program — so generating is what
keeps the probe honestly tracking the library instead of drifting from a
committed copy.

The theorem at level N is N `LetMatch`es nested **in the arm**, i.e. N
recursive `eval` calls in scrutinee position, each with a continuation:

    nest(0) = Ret(rs)
    nest(k) = LetMatch(0, Arm(nest(k-1), NoArm), Ret(rs))

    theorem: eval(N+1, PNil)(VCons(VCon(0, VNil), VNil), nest(N)) = Out(…)

The two families differ in `rs` alone, and the difference is the whole
finding:

  nest  rs = RCons(0, RNil) — each level's continuation REBINDS the output of
        the nested eval and returns it. Exponential on master.
  flat  rs = RNil — same nesting, same step count, but nothing consumes the
        nested eval's payload. Linear on master. This is the negative control
        (docs/testing-generated-terms.md axis 4): it holds nesting depth
        fixed against the one thing that turned out to matter.

Fuel is exactly N+1, the nest's depth; unused fuel is free (measured) but
exact fuel keeps the level index meaning one thing.

The environment is the same one slot at every level — `append(VNil, env)` is
`env` — so the values carried are constant-size across the sweep. The
exponent is therefore not payload growth, which is the issue's finding
restated inside the instrument.


THE MASTER CURVE (2026-07-31, 4300b31, release, `--max 15 --family both`)
=========================================================================

Declarations-only baseline: 0.57 s wall, 9.9 MB peak RSS. Both columns are
baseline-subtracted; `ratio` is against the previous level, `·` marks a level
whose predecessor is under the noise floor.

   N   nest Δwall  ratio    nest ΔRSS  ratio  |  flat Δwall  flat ΔRSS
   1       0.06 s    ·          0.0 MB   ·    |     0.18 s      0.0 MB
   2       0.09 s    ·          0.0 MB   ·    |     0.07 s      0.0 MB
   3       0.13 s    ·          0.5 MB   ·    |     0.09 s      0.0 MB
   4       0.28 s    ·          2.5 MB   ·    |     0.14 s      0.1 MB
   5       0.21 s   0.75        6.4 MB  2.57  |     0.17 s      0.5 MB
   6       0.29 s    ·         13.4 MB  2.09  |     0.20 s      1.0 MB
   7       0.36 s   1.25       27.3 MB  2.04  |     0.23 s      1.2 MB
   8       0.50 s   1.41       45.0 MB  1.65  |     0.26 s      1.6 MB
   9       0.71 s   1.42       90.3 MB  2.00  |     0.31 s      1.9 MB
  10       1.34 s   1.87      118.9 MB  1.32  |     0.36 s      2.1 MB
  11       2.56 s   1.91      226.7 MB  1.91  |     0.38 s      2.7 MB
  12       5.20 s   2.03      500.8 MB  2.21  |     0.42 s      3.2 MB
  13       9.29 s   1.79     1040.1 MB  2.08  |     0.45 s      4.1 MB
  14      16.89 s   1.82     2159.3 MB  2.08  |     0.50 s      4.7 MB
  15      33.77 s   2.00     4543.8 MB  2.10  |     0.56 s      5.2 MB

N = 15 is the last level that fits the 8 GB rail; N = 16 dies on it. The
whole sweep is about two and a half minutes.

Gate (design doc §13 stage 0: ratio ≥ 1.8 across ≥ 4 consecutive levels) —
**held, on both quantities**:

  * peak RSS: five consecutive levels at ratio ≥ 1.8 (N = 11..15), tail
    geometric mean **2.12**. RSS is the cleaner signal at small N because it
    is not baseline-dominated: it is already doubling per level from N = 5.
  * wall time: three consecutive at ≥ 1.8 (N = 10..12) with N = 13 at 1.79,
    tail geometric mean **1.91** over N = 11..15. Per-level wall ratios wander
    ±30% with machine load — a second sweep of the same binary put the run at
    N = 11..12 and the soft level at 13, a third at N = 9..12 with 13 soft —
    so the geometric mean is the statistic to quote and the run is the
    statistic to sanity-check it against.
  * control: `flat` never reaches 1.8 anywhere on either quantity across
    fifteen levels; tail geometric means 1.10 (wall) and 1.18 (RSS). It is
    linear at ~0.04 s and ~0.4 MB per level.

So both quantities carry the ~×2 per level the issue measured on subst
node-visits, in a probe that needs no kernel instrumentation to see it, and
the control says the sweep is measuring nesting rather than file size.

**What the sweep additionally establishes**, and it sharpens the issue's
diagnosis: three probe variants were built and only one is exponential.

  | variant                                              | N=12 peak RSS |
  | ---------------------------------------------------- | ------------- |
  | nesting only (`flat`)                                 | 13 MB         |
  | nesting + a scrutinee value that deepens per level     | 13 MB         |
  | nesting + the continuation rebinding the Out payload   | 511 MB        |

Nesting alone is not sufficient (which is §12's secondary finding, now
reproduced at CLI level), and a growing scrutinee payload is not it either.
What multiplies is a nested eval whose *result is consumed by the pending
continuation* — the continuation is what gets copied per level, exactly the
surviving hypothesis in the issue.

Acceptance for the fix (stage 2b): `--expect linear` passes on `nest`.
"""

import argparse
import os
import resource
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from pathlib import Path

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
LIB = ROOT / "lib" / "ir_v1.hthr"
HEATHER = ROOT / "target" / "release" / "heather"

# The CLAUDE.md rail, as rlimits on the child.
AS_BYTES = 8388608 * 1024  # ulimit -v 8388608 (KiB)
CPU_SECONDS = 120  # ulimit -t 120

# The gate of design doc §13 stage 0.
GATE_RATIO = 1.8
GATE_RUN = 4

# Noise floors on the previous level's baseline-subtracted cost. Below these
# the ratio is a quotient of two rounding errors — at small N the 0.55 s /
# 9 MB declarations baseline dominates the wall clock entirely, and the
# allocator's page granularity dominates RSS. Ratios computed from a
# below-floor predecessor are printed as `·` and never counted by the gate.
FLOOR_WALL = 0.25  # seconds
FLOOR_RSS = 2.0  # MB


def declarations() -> str:
    """`lib/certgen/ir_v1.hthr` up to its first theorem — the same cut the elab test
    makes. A probe file is these declarations plus one generated theorem."""
    text = LIB.read_text()
    cut = text.find("\ntheorem ")
    if cut < 0:
        sys.exit("error: lib/certgen/ir_v1.hthr has no theorems; the cut is wrong")
    return text[:cut]


def nest(depth: int, rs: str) -> str:
    ir = f"Ret({rs})"
    for _ in range(depth):
        ir = f"LetMatch(0, Arm({ir}, NoArm), Ret({rs}))"
    return ir


def theorem(family: str, n: int) -> str:
    if family == "nest":
        rs, out = "RCons(0, RNil)", "Out(VCons(VCon(0, VNil), VNil))"
    else:
        rs, out = "RNil", "Out(VNil)"
    return (
        f"theorem probe_{family}_{n}():\n"
        f"    eval({n + 1}, PNil)(VCons(VCon(0, VNil), VNil),\n"
        f"        {nest(n, rs)})\n"
        f"    = {out}\n"
        f"{{ refl }}\n"
    )


def write_probe(out_dir: Path, decls: str, family: str, n: int) -> Path:
    path = out_dir / f"{family}_{n}.hthr"
    path.write_text(f"{decls}\n{theorem(family, n)}")
    return path


def run(path: Path):
    """One `heather check` under the rail. Returns (wall, cpu, peak_rss_mb,
    status). `os.wait4` is what makes the rusage per-run: RUSAGE_CHILDREN
    accumulates a maximum and would report the largest run seen so far."""
    t0 = time.monotonic()
    pid = os.fork()
    if pid == 0:  # child
        try:
            resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_AS, (AS_BYTES, AS_BYTES))
            resource.setrlimit(resource.RLIMIT_CPU, (CPU_SECONDS, CPU_SECONDS))
            devnull = os.open(os.devnull, os.O_WRONLY)
            os.dup2(devnull, 1)
            os.dup2(devnull, 2)
            os.execv(str(HEATHER), [str(HEATHER), "check", str(path)])
        except BaseException:
            os._exit(127)
    _, status, ru = os.wait4(pid, 0)
    wall = time.monotonic() - t0
    return (
        wall,
        ru.ru_utime + ru.ru_stime,
        ru.ru_maxrss / 1024.0,
        status,
    )


def describe(status: int) -> str:
    if os.WIFSIGNALED(status):
        return f"killed by signal {os.WTERMSIG(status)} (the rail bit)"
    code = os.WEXITSTATUS(status)
    return "ok" if code == 0 else f"exit {code}"


def sweep(out_dir: Path, decls: str, family: str, levels, base):
    print(f"\n=== {family} " + ("(the probe)" if family == "nest" else "(the control)"))
    print(
        "   N        wall         cpu      peak RSS    "
        "|  Δwall  ratio    ΔRSS  ratio   status"
    )
    rows = []
    for n in levels:
        path = write_probe(out_dir, decls, family, n)
        wall, cpu, rss, status = run(path)
        d_wall = wall - base[0]
        d_rss = rss - base[2]
        pw = rows[-1][1] if rows else None
        pr = rows[-1][2] if rows else None
        rw = d_wall / pw if pw is not None and pw >= FLOOR_WALL else None
        rr = d_rss / pr if pr is not None and pr >= FLOOR_RSS else None
        rows.append((n, d_wall, d_rss, rw, rr))
        fmt = lambda x: f"{x:5.2f}" if x is not None else "    ·"
        print(
            f"{n:4d}  {wall:8.2f}s  {cpu:8.2f}s  {rss:9.1f} MB    "
            f"| {d_wall:6.2f}s {fmt(rw)}  {d_rss:7.1f} {fmt(rr)}   {describe(status)}"
        )
        if os.WIFSIGNALED(status) or os.WEXITSTATUS(status) != 0:
            print("   (stopping this family: the run did not check)")
            break
    return rows


def longest_run(ratios) -> tuple:
    """Longest run of consecutive levels at or above the gate ratio."""
    best = (0, None, None)
    cur = 0
    start = None
    for n, r in ratios:
        if r is not None and r >= GATE_RATIO:
            cur += 1
            start = start if cur > 1 else n
            if cur > best[0]:
                best = (cur, start, n)
        else:
            cur, start = 0, None
    return best


def tail_geomean(rows, idx, floor):
    """Per-level ratio averaged geometrically over the last GATE_RUN+1 levels.

    The per-level ratio is what the gate is stated in, but a single level is
    a noisy estimate of it: the wall clock on this workload wanders ±30% with
    machine load, so an isolated 1.77 next to an isolated 3.21 is one curve,
    not two. The geometric mean over five levels is the same quantity read
    with a longer lever, and it is reported alongside the run so a soft level
    is visible rather than hidden."""
    usable = [r for r in rows if r[idx] >= floor]
    if len(usable) < GATE_RUN + 1:
        return None, None, None
    lo, hi = usable[-(GATE_RUN + 1)], usable[-1]
    span = hi[0] - lo[0]
    if span <= 0 or lo[idx] <= 0:
        return None, None, None
    return (hi[idx] / lo[idx]) ** (1.0 / span), lo[0], hi[0]


def verdict(rows, expect):
    """Print the longest gate-passing run per quantity and judge it.

    The two directions are deliberately asymmetric, each conservative for its
    own claim: "exponential" needs only ONE quantity to reach the run (below
    N ≈ 9 the wall clock is baseline-dominated and cannot, while RSS can),
    "linear" needs BOTH to stay under it (one quantity still doubling is not
    a fixed defect)."""
    runs, means = [], []
    for label, ridx, didx, floor in (("wall", 3, 1, FLOOR_WALL), ("RSS", 4, 2, FLOOR_RSS)):
        run_len, lo, hi = longest_run([(r[0], r[ridx]) for r in rows])
        span = f"N = {lo}..{hi}" if run_len else "nowhere"
        gm, glo, ghi = tail_geomean(rows, didx, floor)
        tail = f"{gm:.2f} over N = {glo}..{ghi}" if gm else "not enough levels above the floor"
        print(
            f"  {label:4}: longest run at ratio ≥ {GATE_RATIO}: "
            f"{run_len} level(s) ({span}); tail geometric mean {tail}"
        )
        runs.append(run_len)
        means.append(gm or 0.0)
    if expect == "exponential":
        return max(runs) >= GATE_RUN or max(means) >= GATE_RATIO
    if expect == "linear":
        if not any(means):
            # No quantity cleared the noise floor for long enough to have an
            # opinion. A short sweep must not be able to certify a fix.
            print(
                f"  (too few levels above the noise floor to judge — "
                f"the master curve needs --max 15)"
            )
            return False
        return max(runs) < GATE_RUN and max(means) < GATE_RATIO
    return True


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description="Arm-nested LetMatch probe for whnf-sharing-term-growth.",
        formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter,
        epilog="See this file's docstring for the shape and the master curve.",
    )
    ap.add_argument("--min", type=int, default=1, help="first level (default 1)")
    ap.add_argument(
        "--max",
        type=int,
        default=14,
        help="last level (default 14; 15 is the last that fits the 8 GB rail)",
    )
    ap.add_argument(
        "--family",
        choices=["nest", "flat", "both"],
        default="nest",
        help="nest = the probe, flat = the negative control (default nest)",
    )
    ap.add_argument(
        "--out",
        type=Path,
        default=ROOT / "target" / "whnf-probe",
        help="where the generated .hthr files go (default target/whnf-probe)",
    )
    ap.add_argument(
        "--expect",
        choices=["exponential", "linear"],
        help=(
            "exit nonzero unless the `nest` family shows (exponential) or does "
            "not show (linear) a run of ≥%d levels at ratio ≥%.1f" % (GATE_RUN, GATE_RATIO)
        ),
    )
    args = ap.parse_args()

    if not HEATHER.is_file():
        sys.exit("error: target/release/heather is missing; run `cargo build --release`")

    args.out.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    decls = declarations()

    base_path = args.out / "base.hthr"
    base_path.write_text(decls)
    base = run(base_path)
    if os.WEXITSTATUS(base[3]) != 0:
        sys.exit(f"error: the declarations-only baseline did not check ({describe(base[3])})")
    print(
        f"baseline (declarations only): {base[0]:.2f}s wall, {base[1]:.2f}s cpu, "
        f"{base[2]:.1f} MB peak RSS"
    )
    print(f"rail: RLIMIT_AS {AS_BYTES // 1024} KiB, RLIMIT_CPU {CPU_SECONDS} s")

    levels = range(args.min, args.max + 1)
    families = ["nest", "flat"] if args.family == "both" else [args.family]
    results = {}
    for family in families:
        results[family] = sweep(args.out, decls, family, levels, base)

    print("\n=== verdict (gate: ratio ≥ %.1f across ≥ %d consecutive levels)" % (GATE_RATIO, GATE_RUN))
    for family in families:
        print(f"{family}:")
        ok = verdict(results[family], args.expect if family == "nest" else None)
        if family == "nest" and args.expect and not ok:
            print(f"\nFAIL: expected the nest family to be {args.expect}.")
            return 1
    if args.expect:
        print(f"\nOK: the nest family is {args.expect}, as expected.")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
